WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.601 --> 00:00:03.601
-Our first guest tonight is
an Emmy Award-winning actor

00:00:03.601 --> 00:00:05.767
you know from "The Birdcage,"
"Brockmire," and "The Simpsons."

00:00:05.767 --> 00:00:08.267
He stars in "Hello Tomorrow!"
which is currently streaming

00:00:08.267 --> 00:00:10.901
on Apple TV+ with new episodes
premiering Fridays.

00:00:10.901 --> 00:00:12.300
Let's take a look.

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:14.801
-This is the moment my whole
life has been leading up to,

00:00:14.801 --> 00:00:17.634
but this...
this is really it.

00:00:17.634 --> 00:00:23.834
♪♪

00:00:23.834 --> 00:00:25.167
-Jesus, Eddie.

00:00:25.167 --> 00:00:27.501
-Because if it's a yes from you
about this,

00:00:27.501 --> 00:00:29.667
I will never win
bigger than that.

00:00:29.667 --> 00:00:31.501
Ever.

00:00:31.501 --> 00:00:32.834
-Lot B-18.

00:00:32.834 --> 00:00:33.901
-Midsize B-plex.

00:00:33.901 --> 00:00:35.400
Full of charm.
Full of charm.

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:37.300
Let's call it a beginning

00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:38.634
of, uh...

00:00:38.634 --> 00:00:40.601
of more than we can
currently know.

00:00:40.601 --> 00:00:41.701
[ Chuckles ]

00:00:41.701 --> 00:00:43.200
-It's great, Eddie.

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:44.200
You did great.

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:46.200
-Um, excuse me?

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:47.868
This is now. This...

00:00:47.868 --> 00:00:50.133
Are we doing this or what?

00:00:50.133 --> 00:00:52.901
-Please welcome back to the show
our friend Hank Azaria.

00:00:52.901 --> 00:00:54.901
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:54.901 --> 00:01:02.367
♪♪

00:01:02.367 --> 00:01:09.767
♪♪

00:01:09.767 --> 00:01:11.767
Welcome back.
-Thank you.

00:01:11.767 --> 00:01:13.334
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:13.334 --> 00:01:14.667
-Hi, everybody.

00:01:14.667 --> 00:01:17.834
-This is a fantastic new show.
Congratulations.

00:01:17.834 --> 00:01:20.167
-Thank you.
-With a pretty trippy premise.

00:01:20.167 --> 00:01:22.067
-Yeah.
-Can you lay it out for us?

00:01:22.067 --> 00:01:23.701
-It's hard to lay out
the premise.

00:01:23.701 --> 00:01:26.868
-It is -- I do think
it's a very difficult task.

00:01:26.868 --> 00:01:29.334
-I've had practice for a year
and I'm still not great at it,

00:01:29.334 --> 00:01:32.667
but it takes place in like
a Jetsons vision of the future.

00:01:32.667 --> 00:01:37.167
Like the future we were promised
in the '50s but never got.

00:01:37.167 --> 00:01:38.834
-Right, floating cars
and that sort of thing.

00:01:38.834 --> 00:01:41.667
-Yeah, jet packs and robots
and things like that.

00:01:41.667 --> 00:01:46.834
And these are con men, salesmen,
selling condos on the moon.

00:01:46.834 --> 00:01:48.167
[ Laughter ]

00:01:48.167 --> 00:01:50.601
Yeah.
You know, that old chestnut.

00:01:50.601 --> 00:01:51.734
[ Laughter ]

00:01:51.734 --> 00:01:54.534
Every year, they bring out
one of these.

00:01:54.534 --> 00:01:57.367
And it was fun.
In the scene you saw,

00:01:57.367 --> 00:01:59.400
you know, these guys...
He's really cynical,

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:00.801
he's a gambling addict,

00:02:00.801 --> 00:02:03.300
and he would sell, you know,
his own mother anything.

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:05.300
And probably has.
-[ Laughs ]

00:02:05.300 --> 00:02:08.801
-And yet he's caught up in
this dream of actually going...

00:02:08.801 --> 00:02:11.133
They were talking about a condo
on the moon that they bought

00:02:11.133 --> 00:02:13.601
and they're really psyched
to get up there and go.

00:02:13.601 --> 00:02:15.767
-You mention
a gambling addiction.

00:02:15.767 --> 00:02:16.934
This is...
-Yes.

00:02:16.934 --> 00:02:18.033
-You've done this before.

00:02:18.033 --> 00:02:19.868
You've played
a gambling addict before.

00:02:19.868 --> 00:02:21.667
-I have played many addicts.
-Yeah.

00:02:21.667 --> 00:02:22.701
[ Laughter ]

00:02:22.701 --> 00:02:24.534
-And I've lived some of it, too.
-Yeah.

00:02:24.534 --> 00:02:26.200
Well, "Brockmire,"
a show I loved --

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:28.734
you were an addicted gambler,
as well.

00:02:28.734 --> 00:02:30.601
So this --
-Well, he was drug and...

00:02:30.601 --> 00:02:32.534
He was addicted to everything.
-Yeah, he was.

00:02:32.534 --> 00:02:34.834
-Sex, drugs, and rock and roll,
baby.

00:02:34.834 --> 00:02:36.701
[ Laughter ]
He was, yeah.

00:02:36.701 --> 00:02:39.868
-But, and you do --
you were a poker player.

00:02:39.868 --> 00:02:41.467
-I still am.
I'm gonna play tonight.

00:02:41.467 --> 00:02:43.300
-You are? You still play?
Good for you.

00:02:43.300 --> 00:02:45.634
-I still play, yes.
-Because we faced off once.

00:02:45.634 --> 00:02:46.968
-I remember.
-Yeah.

00:02:46.968 --> 00:02:48.300
[ Light laughter ]

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:50.634
Celebrity poker.
-"Celebrity Poker Showdown."

00:02:50.634 --> 00:02:52.434
-Yeah. This is years ago.

00:02:52.434 --> 00:02:54.367
This is like
the early 2000 aughts.

00:02:54.367 --> 00:02:56.501
-My buddies Andy Newman
and Josh Malina

00:02:56.501 --> 00:02:58.601
actually produced that show.
-It was a really fun show.

00:02:58.601 --> 00:03:00.667
-Well, you won, if I remember...
-I won the whole thing.

00:03:00.667 --> 00:03:02.167
I should not have.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:02.167 --> 00:03:04.834
-Well, I was...
You know, at that time...

00:03:04.834 --> 00:03:07.167
We played a no-limit
hold 'em tournament.

00:03:07.167 --> 00:03:08.534
-Yep.

00:03:08.534 --> 00:03:12.367
-And I was used to playing
stud poker, but, you know --

00:03:12.367 --> 00:03:13.934
-A lot of excuses
right out of the gate here.

00:03:13.934 --> 00:03:15.167
-Exactly.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:15.167 --> 00:03:17.133
But you remember how bummed
I was when I lost?

00:03:17.133 --> 00:03:18.467
-Yes. Well...
-I was, like, bereft.

00:03:18.467 --> 00:03:20.467
I was really upset.
-There's a photo of both of us.

00:03:20.467 --> 00:03:22.334
And it definitely...
If you just saw the photo,

00:03:22.334 --> 00:03:24.267
you'd be like, "Oh, Hank
definitely is the one

00:03:24.267 --> 00:03:25.534
who's gonna win this."
-Yeah.

00:03:25.534 --> 00:03:27.534
-Look at the two --
I'm just falling to pieces.

00:03:27.534 --> 00:03:29.868
[ Laughter ]
Look how confident you are!

00:03:29.868 --> 00:03:32.300
-I was about to donkey off
all my stack

00:03:32.300 --> 00:03:34.300
to Amy Poehler
right after that.

00:03:34.300 --> 00:03:35.801
-Oh, that's right.
Poehler was in it, as well.

00:03:35.801 --> 00:03:38.133
-Yeah, she took all my money.
-Well, I'm very sorry.

00:03:38.133 --> 00:03:39.167
[ Laughter ]
This, by the way...

00:03:39.167 --> 00:03:40.901
So, I've read
the show described,

00:03:40.901 --> 00:03:43.434
which I think is a very accurate
description of the show --

00:03:43.434 --> 00:03:46.100
"Mad Men" meets "The Jetsons."
-Kind of, yeah.

00:03:46.100 --> 00:03:47.934
-A little "Glengarry Glen Ross"
in there as well, I feel.

00:03:47.934 --> 00:03:49.701
-Definitely.
Con men salesmen.

00:03:49.701 --> 00:03:53.434
-But it's this beautiful,
retro-y '50s look.

00:03:53.434 --> 00:03:55.934
And these are, you know,
one of the robots there.

00:03:55.934 --> 00:03:58.767
-Yeah.
-And it looks practical.

00:03:58.767 --> 00:04:01.667
It does not look CGI.
But of course, there are...

00:04:01.667 --> 00:04:04.667
You know, there is a method
to the madness.

00:04:04.667 --> 00:04:06.634
-Well, yeah,
these robots were real,

00:04:06.634 --> 00:04:07.801
meaning they were there with us.

00:04:07.801 --> 00:04:09.467
They weren't added later.

00:04:09.467 --> 00:04:11.968
But along with these robots

00:04:11.968 --> 00:04:14.501
came these guys
in green unitards...

00:04:14.501 --> 00:04:16.033
[ Laughter ]

00:04:16.033 --> 00:04:18.434
...operating them as puppeteers.

00:04:18.434 --> 00:04:19.868
And including like, yeah --

00:04:19.868 --> 00:04:21.934
-That's a very good picture
of what you're talking about.

00:04:21.934 --> 00:04:23.300
[ Laughter ]
-But, wait.

00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:26.901
That actually was more sane
because they have their...

00:04:26.901 --> 00:04:28.734
When they were operating
in the take,

00:04:28.734 --> 00:04:31.400
they had green masks on, also.
-Right.

00:04:31.400 --> 00:04:33.901
-So they were just, like, these
Mummenschanz green people.

00:04:33.901 --> 00:04:35.400
[ Laughter ]
And you have to like...

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:38.901
I'm there trying to emote with
Emmy Award-winner Billy Crudup.

00:04:38.901 --> 00:04:41.501
And there's these folks
all around you, like, going --

00:04:41.501 --> 00:04:43.300
Nyeh, nyeh, nyeh!

00:04:43.300 --> 00:04:45.634
[ Laughter ]

00:04:45.634 --> 00:04:47.267
Nyeh!

00:04:47.267 --> 00:04:49.634
And, but you get used to it.
-Yeah.

00:04:49.634 --> 00:04:51.267
That, I guess,
is the weirdest thing,

00:04:51.267 --> 00:04:53.501
the day where you realize
you're used to the green people.

00:04:53.501 --> 00:04:55.000
[ Laughter ]

00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:57.734
-It takes 10 minute-- well, all
of show business is like that.

00:04:57.734 --> 00:04:59.234
-Right.
-It really is.

00:04:59.234 --> 00:05:01.200
You know what was one of
the weirdest days of my life?

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:03.734
Like, being on the red carpet --
you know, the red carpet.

00:05:03.734 --> 00:05:06.000
Whatever it was, the Emmys,
whatever it was.

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:09.167
And at first when
you're on that red carpet,

00:05:09.167 --> 00:05:12.234
your, like, heart is pounding
in your throat.

00:05:12.234 --> 00:05:13.901
You're like, "Oh, my God!"

00:05:13.901 --> 00:05:15.234
You can't believe

00:05:15.234 --> 00:05:17.901
you're in the middle
of this iconic, crazy thing.

00:05:17.901 --> 00:05:20.234
And then you cut to
like 15 years later

00:05:20.234 --> 00:05:23.033
and you're literally like,
[sighing] "Ah, man."

00:05:23.033 --> 00:05:24.367
[ Laughter ]

00:05:24.367 --> 00:05:26.200
"Gotta talk to 'ET', I guess.
Whatever.

00:05:26.200 --> 00:05:27.701
Can we get it over with?"
-Yeah.

00:05:27.701 --> 00:05:29.868
-Just feels like...
So, yeah.

00:05:29.868 --> 00:05:33.033
Like, but, shooting
is nuts anyway

00:05:33.033 --> 00:05:36.200
because before every take,
for example,

00:05:36.200 --> 00:05:39.367
you know, makeup comes and
they're doing this to you,

00:05:39.367 --> 00:05:41.334
and hair is doing this.

00:05:41.334 --> 00:05:43.834
And like, it's like chimps
grooming each other.

00:05:43.834 --> 00:05:45.133
[ Laughter ]

00:05:45.133 --> 00:05:47.968
And they, like, pick stuff off
and actually eat it.

00:05:47.968 --> 00:05:49.601
And people will...
You accept...

00:05:49.601 --> 00:05:51.934
In right before
the moment you shoot,

00:05:51.934 --> 00:05:53.434
you'll accept any behavior.

00:05:53.434 --> 00:05:55.467
Like, they could spray-paint
your head.

00:05:55.467 --> 00:05:57.400
-Yeah.
-Take off your clothes.

00:05:57.400 --> 00:05:58.901
Like, "Oh, I guess
I'm naked in this take.

00:05:58.901 --> 00:06:00.300
Whatever."
[ Laughter ]

00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:03.133
And, you know, it's just like...

00:06:03.133 --> 00:06:05.367
-The thing that always
shocked me about acting...

00:06:05.367 --> 00:06:07.701
Obviously, I've done it far less
than you, but you do --

00:06:07.701 --> 00:06:10.667
you think, as an actor,
all the notes are gonna be

00:06:10.667 --> 00:06:12.234
about your performance.
-Yeah.

00:06:12.234 --> 00:06:13.601
-And instead they go, "Cut!"

00:06:13.601 --> 00:06:15.234
and the director's like,
"Move the curtain."

00:06:15.234 --> 00:06:16.968
-Yeah.
-And you realize they're bare--

00:06:16.968 --> 00:06:19.801
You're basically just one of
the other props in the shot.

00:06:19.801 --> 00:06:21.133
[ Laughter ]
-Totally. Totally.

00:06:21.133 --> 00:06:24.300
When you look at dailies,
when people watch and gather,

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:26.367
you know, the hair department
only sees hair.

00:06:26.367 --> 00:06:27.567
-Yeah.

00:06:27.567 --> 00:06:29.734
-The actors only look
at their performance.

00:06:29.734 --> 00:06:32.901
Set Dec only sees
what's going on in the set.

00:06:32.901 --> 00:06:34.934
And you know, I'll do a take
I thought was great,

00:06:34.934 --> 00:06:36.767
and someone will go, "Ugh, God!"

00:06:36.767 --> 00:06:38.534
I'm like, "What's --
What was wrong?"

00:06:38.534 --> 00:06:40.601
Like, "Your hair was like that
in this one."

00:06:40.601 --> 00:06:43.434
[ Laughter ]
I'm like, "Who cares?"

00:06:43.434 --> 00:06:44.934
-Just get...
So, for in the future,

00:06:44.934 --> 00:06:46.934
you should have a couple green
people just watching your hair.

00:06:46.934 --> 00:06:48.767
-Exactly.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:48.767 --> 00:06:50.100
-I have a ton more questions.

00:06:50.100 --> 00:06:51.968
We'll be right back with more
from Hank Azaria.

